<% content_for :title, "#{@world.name}: types" %>
<div class="crumbs"><%= link_to @world.name, world_path(@world) %> / Types</div>
<div class="book-header">
  <h1>Types</h1>
  <div class="actions">
    <% if can_edit_world? %><%= link_to "Edit types", edit_world_types_path(@world), class: "button" %><% end %>
  </div>
</div>
<% if @chart.matter? %>
  <p class="prose">
    Every spell and skill that does damage can have a type, and every monster and job has one. In <%= @world.name %>, a move
    against a target's type can be <strong>super effective</strong> (double damage), <strong>not very effective</strong> (half) or have
    <strong>no effect</strong>. The basic Attack has no type, unless a job gives it one, so it usually lands as it is. A monster can break
    the chart where its Bestiary entry says so, and a character is never immune through their job's type: what the chart calls no effect,
    they resist.
  </p>
  <div class="window type-chart-wrap">
    <table class="type-chart">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Attacking type down the side, defending type along the top</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col"><span class="muted">Move ↓ · Target →</span></th>
          <% @chart.slugs.each do |defending| %><th scope="col"><%= type_tag(defending) %></th><% end %>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% @chart.slugs.each do |attacking| %>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><%= type_tag(attacking) %></th>
            <% @chart.slugs.each do |defending| %>
              <% percent = @chart.percent(attacking, defending) %>
              <% label, klass = { 200 => [ "×2", "is-x2" ], 50 => [ "½", "is-half" ], 0 => [ "0", "is-zero" ] }.fetch(percent, [ "", "" ]) %>
              <td class="<%= klass %>" title="<%= @chart.name(attacking) %> against <%= @chart.name(defending) %>: <%= percent %>%"><%= label %></td>
            <% end %>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <section class="window">
    <h2>Where the fight is</h2>
    <p class="hint">Each terrain has a type: a Geomancer's arts take it. Anywhere else is <%= @chart.name(@chart.plain) %>.</p>
    <dl class="facts facts--wide">
      <% EncounterTable::TERRAINS.each do |place| %>
        <dt><%= place.capitalize %></dt><dd><%= type_tag(@world.terrain_type(place)) %></dd>
      <% end %>
    </dl>
    <% shrugs = @chart.types.select { |t| t.shrugs_off.any? } %>
    <% if shrugs.any? %>
      <h3>Shrugs off</h3>
      <ul class="refs">
        <% shrugs.each do |t| %><li><%= type_tag(t.slug) %> can't be <%= t.shrugs_off.map { |s| term(s).downcase }.to_sentence(two_words_connector: " or ", last_word_connector: " or ") %></li><% end %>
      </ul>
    <% end %>
  </section>
<% else %>
  <p class="prose">
    <%= @world.name %> has one type, <%= @chart.name(@chart.plain) %>: every blow lands as it is, and nothing about types shows up in its
    books or at the table. A monster can still be the exception, where its Bestiary entry says it shrugs off blows or drinks them.
    <% if can_edit_world? %>Add types to give the setting its own chart.<% end %>
  </p>
<% end %>
